title: Changing a field's type
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: guide
summary: mf retype converts every value of a field to another type, in one write you can roll back; a value that cannot convert is removed and traced in retype_lost on its metarecord.

A field name's type is fixed by its first write ([[One value type per field name]]).
To change it, ''retype'' the field — every value of that name, in the whole
repository:

```
mf -n music retype rating float
retyped rating to float: 1204 value(s) converted, 0 metarecord(s) lost a value that did not convert
mf -n music retype code int
retyped code to int: 310 value(s) converted, 2 metarecord(s) lost a value that did not convert, traced in retype_lost
```

Any type can be reached from any other: a mistyped field is never stuck. The
retype is one logged write, so `mf log undo` takes it back. Reserved names
(`mfr_*`, `mf_*`) cannot be retyped: the system owns their types.

Each distinct value is converted on all its holders at once, a mass remove and a mass
add ([[Mass writes in the store]]): the values are read record by record, the writes
are not. A `tree_ref` or `hierarchy_ref` — the target, or a value of a metarecord — is
converted one metarecord at a time, its forest checked.

!! How values convert

* ''To `string`'' — always, by the value's canonical text: numbers and booleans as text, dates as ISO-8601, `ref`/`refbase` as a 32-hex uuid, `tree_ref` as `<parent-hex>/<name>` (`/<name>` for a root), `externalref` as `<repo-hex>:<metarecord-hex>`. Losslessly, with two exceptions the text cannot carry: a date's milliseconds and an instant before 1970 (ISO-8601 here has whole seconds from the epoch on), and a name that is not UTF-8, which becomes its shown form, `%XX` escapes included ([[Tree names]]) — converting back reads those escapes as characters.
* ''From `string`'' — by parsing that same form (surrounding spaces ignored). `string` is the hub: to reinterpret a value as another reference type, go through it.
* ''Between scalars'' — `int`, `float`, `bool` and `datetime` convert between each other (a float to an int truncates; a bool is `0`/`1`; a date is its Unix milliseconds; a number is true unless zero), except a `bool` to a `datetime`.
* ''Between `tree_ref` and `hierarchy_ref''' — as they are: every tree is a hierarchy ([[Hierarchy references]]). Back to `tree_ref`, a node keeps its first parent and the others fall back, being second positions.
* ''Between two other reference types'', or between a reference and a scalar other than `string` — no conversion.

A value that does not convert is ''removed''; when none of a metarecord's values of the
field converts, the field goes from it — it is then unknown, never `nothing`, since
`nothing` would say the metarecord is known to have no value, when it most likely has
one that could not be converted ([[Nothing versus unknown]]). No default stands in for
it: a `0` or a `""` made up would read as a real value. The command counts them;
`POST /retype` also lists their metarecords (`fallback_uuids`), and `mf log undo` gives
them back.

!! Finding what a retype lost

Each lost value leaves a trace on its metarecord: a `retype_lost` string,
`<field> = <value>`, the value in the canonical text of ''To `string`'' above —
`code = oops`, `cat = 8f3a…/leaf`. The field is multi-valued and kept across
retypes, so a query finds them whenever it is asked:

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'retype_lost IS PRESENT' get
mf -n music metarecord -q 'retype_lost IS PRESENT' field unset retype_lost
```

It is a ''user'' field — no prefix, as `orphan` ([[Finding and clearing orphans]]):
a trace to read, fix and clear, which takes no `--force`. It is written in the
retype's own write, so `mf log undo` takes it back with the retype. A
repository already using `retype_lost` with another type gets the ordinary
type-lock error, and the retype is refused rather than losing a value without
a trace. Retyping `retype_lost` itself traces nothing: its lost values would
not be of its new type. A `tree_ref` that would break its forest ([[TreeRef forest]]) — a missing
parent, a cycle, a second position — is removed too, so one bad path never blocks the
rest. A `nothing` stays `nothing`: absence has no type to convert. Two values of one metarecord that the conversion
makes equal become one row ([[No duplicate rows]]).
